  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 AM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nThrough Wednesday: An upper shortwave currently situated over the\nMid South will shift eastward into the Tennessee Valley today. \nThe trailing portion of this wave, extending across parts of \nNorth MS to near the AR/LA border, will serve as a focus for\nscattered convective development later today into early this \nevening. Further south, typical warm season isolated to scattered \ndiurnal convection is expected. As we head into early next week, \nupper level ridging will begin to limit convection with afternoon\nshowers and storms becoming more isolated.\n\nWith the building ridge, temps will trend upward again, with highs \nin the triple digits possible across the Delta region Monday \nthrough Wednesday. This airmass will remain seasonably humid, \nthough we expect to see increasing daytime mixing as we head into\nnext week with dewpoints dropping into the 60s during the \nafternoon hours over much of the area. Still with conditions \nbeing so warm, heat indices will approach critical thresholds as \nearly as today across northwest MS/north LA/southeast AR and over\nmuch of the western half of the forecast area by tomorrow. For \ntoday, it is expected heat advisory criteria will only be met \nbriefly in a few areas. However, advisories may be warranted \nlater on for a portion of our area. We will continue to highlight \nthe increasing heat stress concerns in graphical products. \n\nThursday through next Saturday: By mid week, low level flow will \nbecome increasingly easterly thanks to the orientation of the \nsurface high, ushering in a relatively drier airmass. This airmass \nwill still support hot days, but with dewpoints dropping into the \n60s - perhaps even the 50s as times during daytime mixing - it could \ngreatly alleviate heat stress danger through the end of the forecast \nperiod. Deep layer moisture will begin to increase again late Friday \ninto next Saturday, with returning potential for diurnal convection. \n/DL/",
